---
import DocShell from '../../layouts/doc-shell.astro';
import { docsLinks } from '../../lib/nav';

const toc = [
   { id: 'sources', label: 'Upstream sources' },
   { id: 'artifacts', label: 'Generated artifacts' },
   { id: 'criterion', label: 'A criterion record' },
   { id: 'refresh', label: 'Refreshing the data' },
];
---

<DocShell
   title="WCAG data"
   summary="a11ied ships WCAG 2.1 and 2.2 as generated artifacts inside the package. Criterion lookup, search, coverage, and applicability all read those files, so none of them makes a network request at runtime."
   links={docsLinks}
   toc={toc}
>
   <section class="ledger" id="sources">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Upstream</p>
         <h2>Upstream sources</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
         The Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) are the standard most accessibility
         requirements come from. WCAG states its rules as success criteria: numbered, testable
         requirements such as 1.4.3, each assigned a conformance level of A, AA, or AAA that says
         how strict the requirement is. a11ied pins four upstream sources for WCAG 2.1 and 2.2
         and merges them into one record per criterion.
      </p>
      <div class="route-list">
         <div class="route-row">
            <strong>W3C WCAG 2.1 and 2.2</strong>
            <span>Criterion IDs, titles, levels, normative text, techniques, and failures.</span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <strong>W3C ACT rule mapping</strong>
            <span>Machine-readable rule to criterion coverage from the Accessibility Conformance Testing (ACT) rules.</span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <strong>W3C Quickref tags</strong>
            <span>Topic tags per criterion. Applicability matches page signals against these.</span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <strong>axe-core</strong>
            <span>Rule IDs, WCAG tags, and ACT rule IDs read from the installed package. axe-core (axe) is an open-source rules engine, not something this project built. It runs inside the page and checks it against a set of automated accessibility rules.</span>
         </div>
      </div>
      <p>
         Coverage says how much of a criterion a11ied's own checks can resolve without a person,
         from <code>automated</code> to <code>manual</code>. See <a href="/test-methods">coverage</a>
         for the four states. Each record lists the sources that produced it under
         <code>sourceAttribution</code>, so you can tell an ACT mapping from a heuristic.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="artifacts">
      <h2>Generated artifacts</h2>
      <p>
         The sync step fetches the raw upstream payloads into an ignored directory, then
         generates normalized artifacts per version. The generated set is committed and ships
         in the package, so a build never reaches the network.
      </p>
      <div class="route-list">
         <div class="route-row"><code>criteria</code><span>Full criterion records with techniques and failures.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>criteria-by-level</code><span>Criterion IDs grouped by A, AA, and AAA.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>test-methods</code><span>Test method (<code>method</code>) and mapped rule IDs per criterion.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>test-method-summary</code><span>Count of automated, hybrid, and manual criteria per level, source counts, and representative IDs.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>strategy</code><span>Preferred test method, procedure IDs, and whether the page must be open in a browser or screen reader (<code>requiresRealTarget</code>).</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>slug-index</code><span>Slug to criterion ID. Every lookup by slug reads it.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><div class="route-terms"><code>technique-index</code>, <code>failure-index</code></div><span>Technique or failure ID to its title, W3C page URL, and the criteria that list it. <code>a1 wcag G18</code> reads them.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>axe-rules</code><span>axe rule ID to its tags, ACT rule IDs, and the criteria it maps to. <code>a1 wcag rule</code> reads it.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>understanding</code><span>Each criterion's Understanding document, converted to Markdown, with its title, URL, and status. <code>a1 wcag understanding</code> reads it.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>technique-bodies</code><span>Each technique and failure's page body, converted to Markdown. <code>a1 wcag G18</code> reads it when one was synced.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>documents-content</code><span>The converted document bodies, one copy per distinct body, shared by <code>understanding</code> and <code>technique-bodies</code>.</span></div>
      </div>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="criterion">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Shape</p>
         <h2>A criterion record</h2>
      </div>
      <pre><code>a1 wcag show 1.4.3 --json</code></pre>
      <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>"result": &#123;
  "lookupKey": "1.4.3",
  "criterion": &#123;
    "id": "1.4.3",
    "slug": "contrast-minimum",
    "title": "Contrast (Minimum)",
    "level": "AA",
    "wcagVersion": "2.2",
    "summary": "The visual presentation of text and images of text has a contrast ratio of at least 4.5:1, except for the following:",
    "understandingUrl": "https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/contrast-minimum",
    "versions": ["2.0", "2.1", "2.2"],
    "tags": ["buttons", "color", "content", "contrast", "errors", "focus", "forms", "images-of-text", "messaging", "navigation", "text"],
    "principle": &#123; "id": "perceivable", "number": "1", "title": "Perceivable" &#125;,
    "guideline": &#123; "id": "distinguishable", "number": "1.4", "title": "Distinguishable" &#125;
  &#125;,
  "testMethod": &#123; "criterionId": "1.4.3", "method": "automated", "..." : "..." &#125;,
  "strategy": &#123; "criterionId": "1.4.3", "preferredEvidenceMode": "automated", "..." : "..." &#125;
&#125;</code></pre>
      <p>
         <code>result.criterion</code> also includes <code>normativeText</code> as HTML, a parsed
         <code>details</code> list for the exceptions, <code>altIds</code> for older slugs, and
         the resolved <code>techniques</code>, <code>advisoryTechniques</code>, and
         <code>failures</code>. Each technique has an <code>id</code>, a <code>title</code>, a
         <code>technology</code>, and a <code>url</code> to its W3C page.
      </p>
      <p>
         <code>versions</code> says which WCAG releases contain the criterion. See
         <a href="/test-methods">test methods</a> for what <code>result.testMethod</code> and
         <code>result.strategy</code> hold.
      </p>
      <p>
         The Understanding document and the technique body are in the data too, converted from
         HTML to Markdown and stored under <code>understanding.&lt;version&gt;.json</code> and
         <code>technique-bodies.&lt;version&gt;.json</code>. <code>a1 wcag understanding 1.4.3</code>
         prints the full document, and <code>a1 wcag show 1.4.3</code> prints its In Brief or
         Intent opening with a link to the rest.
      </p>
      <p>
         <code>understandingUrl</code> and a technique's <code>url</code> still go to the
         original W3C page. The W3C Document License permits this conversion to help
         implementation, and the package ships a <code>NOTICE.md</code> with the required W3C
         attribution. Every command that prints this material also prints a line that gives the
         source document's title and URL.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="field-note" id="refresh">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Maintenance</p>
         <h2>Refreshing the data</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
         From the repository root, <code>npm run wcag:sync</code> pulls the upstream payloads and
         regenerates the artifacts. <code>npm run wcag:validate</code> checks them against the
         provenance manifests and the regression fixtures.
      </p>
      <p>
         Review the generated diff before committing. Both commands are for maintainers. Nothing in
         normal use of the CLI, MCP server, or API triggers a sync.
      </p>
   </section>
</DocShell>
